A||N||7||jlhuber||rocker arm question||||5||
Z||000000||jlhuber||01-16-2000||07:32 PM||||how long would it normally take to adjust all the rocker arms for all 6 cylinders on the 2.8?  the engine is still in the car, so i am guessing that i am going to have a little trouble due to clearence, or lack thereof that is.  i am also guessing that it is going to be harder then just poping off the valve covers and tightening down the nuts, right?  what all is involved in this... uh... task?  i don't have a manual anymore for i used to have the haynes manual but it pissed me off so i threw it in the trash, and i can't find a store locally that sells the chiltons yet.  any help would be great guys, and gals.  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||mwbackus||01-16-2000||08:37 PM||||Good News!!  The V6 uses hydraulic lifters and they are "self-adjusting".  A "very" nice feature, since the front valve cover will not clear the rocker assembly without removing the intake plenum.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000002||Greg Piet||01-16-2000||08:47 PM||||Well i have been working on doing my valve lash (adjust rocker arms) this weekend(1985 2.8).  Here is what I had to take off to get to the top valve cover bolts.  I also used a 8mm wrench to remove the back pass side bolt so i didnt have to remove the motor mount brackets.<BR>Also to remove the front valve cover w/o removing mounts I put the motor in the #4 firing position, it was tight but it can be done.<P>Removed from car:<BR>Upper intake plenum and egr tube<BR>Plug wires<BR>Vaccum line assembly <BR>injector rail (including the cold start injector wire)<BR>middle plenum<P>Working carefully I could disconnect the cruise and accel cable, and leave the butterfly assembly where it was.  I also left the sensor wires alone.<P>I can now do it pretty quickly but the first time took alot longer. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||01-17-2000||01:29 AM||||I took a valve cover and cut the center out. I bolt it on and adjust them with the engine running. GOD DON'T REV IT!||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||01-17-2000||02:25 AM||||terryk,<P>That is a good idea....it really comes in handy.<P>As for removing half the engine to remove the valve covers....you're NUTS!  They can be removed without taking off the plenum.  You will need to remove the dogbone and it's brackets, but that is all.  I have done this many times and it isn't too difficult.<P>You will need a 1/4" drive ratchet with a "U" joint and some extensions to go with it.  With the "U" joint you can get to the "hidden" bolts under the plenum.  Once those are removed you can take off the valve cover.  It does require a bit of manuevering to get it off (you have to get over the rocker arms), but it is still much easier than removing the plenum.  ||||reg||4
Z||000005||jlhuber||01-17-2000||07:01 PM||||thanx to those who replied.  now whats with this #4 firing position?  i know what it is, but my question is why?  and do i leave it in that position for all the cylinders, or change it for each one?  TIA||||reg||1
Z||000006||Harry||01-17-2000||11:02 PM||||No, just moves the rokers out of your way to pull the cover (when not removing the plenum)||||reg||12
Z||000007||cscougar||01-18-2000||10:32 PM||||MrFiero<P>Do you get to the hidden rocker cover bolts through the phenum with the sockets?  I am going to have to take my rear rocker cover off this weekend and am not looking forward in removing the phenum and all.<P>Thanks,<P>Ted||||reg||14
